WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.899 --> 00:00:04.238
- [Narrator] In the 1920s
and up through the 1950s,  

00:00:04.238 --> 00:00:07.447
Hollywood had a particular way of talking.

00:00:07.447 --> 00:00:09.387
- Come around about noon tomorrow.

00:00:09.387 --> 00:00:11.551
- What are up doing up there,
impersonating a book cover?

00:00:11.551 --> 00:00:12.593
- Really?

00:00:12.593 --> 00:00:14.082
- Really.

00:00:14.082 --> 00:00:15.352
- [Narrator] It was called the mid-Atlantic

00:00:15.352 --> 00:00:17.447
or transatlantic accent.

00:00:17.447 --> 00:00:19.551
But we don't speak like that anymore.

00:00:19.551 --> 00:00:20.968
So, what happened?

00:00:22.419 --> 00:00:25.983
To know why it disappeared,
we need a little history.

00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:29.566
The accent is sort of a
British American hybrid.

00:00:29.566 --> 00:00:33.533
Linguistically speaking,
it has three main tenants.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:37.342
One, drop the R, like mothah and fathah.

00:00:37.342 --> 00:00:41.092
Two, emphasize the T,
like writah and wintah.

00:00:42.056 --> 00:00:47.055
And three, soften your
vowels, like daance and caah.

00:00:47.055 --> 00:00:50.326
And this accent was
acquired, so there's no line

00:00:50.326 --> 00:00:53.314
tracing it back through history.

00:00:53.314 --> 00:00:55.319
Some believe it came out of early radio

00:00:55.319 --> 00:00:57.649
when base tones were difficult to hear.

00:00:57.649 --> 00:01:01.231
But many attribute its popularity
to writer Edith Skinner.

00:01:01.231 --> 00:01:03.660
She actually wrote a book
called Speak with Distinction,

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.306
which was the book to
help you learn the accent.

00:01:07.306 --> 00:01:10.280
Either way, it was a language
for the American upper class.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:13.423
People like FDR, Katharine
Hepburn, Cary Grant,

00:01:13.423 --> 00:01:14.961
they all used it.

00:01:14.961 --> 00:01:19.116
But after World War II, the
accent dwindled in popularity.

00:01:19.116 --> 00:01:21.555
With a growing middle class,
an aristocratic accent

00:01:21.555 --> 00:01:23.229
was no longer fashionable.

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:26.212
As for Hollywood, acting methods changed,

00:01:26.212 --> 00:01:28.749
and the accent was just too fake.

00:01:28.749 --> 00:01:30.410
So, it disappeared.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:31.243
Mostly.

00:01:32.288 --> 00:01:34.189
If you've ever seen the show Frasier,

00:01:34.189 --> 00:01:37.576
Niles and Frasier spoke with
an updated mid-Atlantic accent.

00:01:37.576 --> 00:01:39.526
- To let the woman I love
die before your eyes.

00:01:39.526 --> 00:01:40.985
That's right, I said I love her!

00:01:40.985 --> 00:01:42.248
(audience laughing)

00:01:42.248 --> 00:01:43.915
- [Narrator] Lovely.

00:01:45.236 --> 00:01:47.819
(upbeat music)

